From b3e7e496b95666b1b3885a565e774b2464ee16cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brown25 <asetium@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 16 Jul 2026 11:11:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rxrpc: Also unshare DATA packets when paged frags are present

CVE-2026-43500 affects encrypted RxRPC packets that carry a frag list
or externally owned paged fragments.  Such an skb may not be cloned,
so skb_unshare() can return the original skb even though RxKAD will
subsequently bind its fragment pages into a scatterlist and decrypt
them in place.

This tree predates the newer rxrpc_input_call_event() structure.  Its
equivalent encrypted DATA handling is in rxrpc_input_packet() in
net/rxrpc/input.c, where skb_unshare() is called before the packet is
passed to the RxKAD verification path.

Force a full skb_copy() when skb_has_frag_list() or
skb_has_shared_frag() is true.  Preserve the ownership semantics of
skb_unshare() by consuming the original skb on success and freeing it
on allocation failure.  The existing RxRPC skb tracing and
out-of-memory handling are then reused unchanged.

This adapts upstream commit aa54b1d27fe0c2b78e664a34fd0fdf7cd1960d71
("rxrpc: Also unshare DATA/RESPONSE packets when paged frags are
present") to the older RxRPC architecture.

The RESPONSE part of the upstream change is not required in this tree.
rxkad_verify_response() copies the response structure and ticket out
of the skb with skb_copy_bits() and performs decryption on those
private allocations rather than decrypting the RESPONSE skb or its
fragments in place.

The zero-copy path remains unchanged for encrypted DATA skbs whose
fragments are private to the kernel.

Fixes: d0d5c0cd1e71 ("rxrpc: Use skb_unshare() rather than skb_cow_data()")
CVE: CVE-2026-43500
Signed-off-by: brown25 <asetium@gmail.com>
---
 net/rxrpc/input.c | 13 ++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/net/rxrpc/input.c b/net/rxrpc/input.c
index 721d847ba..99c4a1969 100644
--- a/net/rxrpc/input.c
+++ b/net/rxrpc/input.c
@@ -1314,7 +1314,18 @@ int rxrpc_input_packet(struct sock *udp_sk, struct sk_buff *skb)
 		 * decryption.
 		 */
 		if (sp->hdr.securityIndex != 0) {
-			struct sk_buff *nskb = skb_unshare(skb, GFP_ATOMIC);
+			struct sk_buff *nskb;
+
+			if (skb_has_frag_list(skb) || skb_has_shared_frag(skb)) {
+				nskb = skb_copy(skb, GFP_ATOMIC);
+				if (likely(nskb))
+					consume_skb(skb);
+				else
+					kfree_skb(skb);
+			} else {
+				nskb = skb_unshare(skb, GFP_ATOMIC);
+			}
+
 			if (!nskb) {
 				rxrpc_eaten_skb(skb, rxrpc_skb_unshared_nomem);
 				goto out;
